1072 #uuid "657c51ff-9f17-50fc-8c5e-6121474d9102" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479093 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write the number as a regular fraction:\n\n$$\n10\\,\\frac{5}{8}=\\frac{10\\cdot 8+5}{8}=\\frac{85}{8}\n$$\n\nThen, we flip the numerator and denominator, keeping the sign of the fraction. So the reciprocal of $10\\,\\frac{5}{8}$ is $\\frac{8}{85}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479100 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479052 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479100 #edb/ref 1069 "q-178518" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479100 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479100 #edb/ref 1071 "Expressed as a **fraction in its simplest form**, the reciprocal of $-7.8$ is{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479100 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "807be93b-6cd0-5b9f-b7c9-fdc2729b89b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479100 #edb/ref 1074 "We first convert the decimal to a fraction. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n-7.8=-\\frac{78}{10}=-\\frac{39}{5}.\n$$\n\nNow, we flip the numerator and denominator, keeping the negative sign. So the reciprocal of $-7.8$ is $-\\frac{5}{39}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479107 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479107 #edb/ref 1069 "q-43434" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479107 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479107 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the reciprocal of $131?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479107 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8900c7d1-2a28-502b-a98f-1385a152c2ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479107 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the reciprocal of $131$ we divide $1$ by $131$, keeping the sign. So, we obtain \n\n$$\n1÷131=\\frac{1}{131}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479108 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479108 #edb/ref 1069 "q-173695" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479108 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479108 #edb/ref 1071 "The reciprocal of $-4\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ is{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479108 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "91c3c8c6-ac05-5f5c-bd08-a6bb467209d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479108 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write the number as a regular fraction:\n\n$$\n-4\\,\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{4\\cdot 2+1}{2}=-\\frac{9}{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we flip the numerator and denominator, keeping the sign of the fraction. So the reciprocal of $-4\\frac{1}{2}$ is $-\\frac{2}{9}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479129 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479129 #edb/ref 1069 "q-99730" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479129 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479129 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the reciprocal of $-\\frac{1}{13}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479129 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cd9e1cbb-b0df-5eb6-9851-fa54454509dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479129 #edb/ref 1074 "We flip the numerator and denominator, keeping the sign of the fraction. So the reciprocal of $-\\frac{1}{13}$ is  $-\\frac{13}{1}=-13.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479135 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479135 #edb/ref 1069 "q-43194" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479135 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479135 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the reciprocal of $-13?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479135 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "df27d9b5-003c-5fda-b194-15d33695bb2d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479135 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the reciprocal of $-13$ we divide $1$ by $-13$, keeping the sign. So, we obtain \n\n$$\n1÷(-13)=\\frac{1}{-13}=-\\frac{1}{13}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479142 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479142 #edb/ref 1069 "q-178342" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479142 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479142 #edb/ref 1071 "The reciprocal of $4\\,\\frac{2}{3}$ is{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479142 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f127ccf9-f581-5522-a0c1-5f3908dc1d25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479142 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write the number as a regular fraction:\n\n$$\n4\\,\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{4\\cdot 3+2}{3}=\\frac{14}{3}\n$$\n\nThen, we flip the numerator and denominator, keeping the sign of the fraction. So the reciprocal of $\\frac{14}{3}$ is $\\frac{3}{14}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
